      <Row>
        <Cell>
          <Data ss:Type="Number"><![CDATA[1]]></Data>
        </Cell>
        <Cell>
          <Data ss:Type="String"><![CDATA[9/17/2026 3:45:07 AM +00:00]]></Data>
        </Cell>
        <Cell>
          <Data ss:Type="String"><![CDATA[10/29/2026 8:02:27 AM +00:00]]></Data>
        </Cell>
        <Cell>
          <Data ss:Type="String"><![CDATA[Evaluating the Impact of Pre-Registration Systems on Conference Review Dynamics: An Empirical Study on Microsoft CMT Workflows]]></Data>
        </Cell>
        <Cell>
          <Data ss:Type="String"><![CDATA[Peer-review platforms form the operational backbone of scholarly publishing, yet balancing reviewer workload with domain-specific expertise remains a persistent challenge in academic conference management. This study examines the efficacy of automated workflow configurations within the Microsoft Conference Management Toolkit (CMT), specifically analyzing how dual-stage pre-registration and subject-category bidding influence the distribution and precision of paper assignments. Utilizing a simulated track dataset comprising 250 submissions across five specialized sub-domains, we model submission dynamics, conflict-of-interest (CoI) detection accuracy, and reviewer workload variance.

Our empirical evaluation focuses on three primary metrics: bid-to-assignment alignment, time-to-decision latency, and the incidence of unhandled domain mismatches. The results indicate that integrating automated primary/secondary subject area constraints with explicit CoI filtering reduces cross-track assignment errors by 34% compared to baseline manual allocations. Furthermore, automated track chair overrides and meta-reviewer oversight loops significantly lower review disparity across multi-track settings without increasing administrative overhead. These findings demonstrate that optimizing Microsoft CMT parameterization enhances reviewer match quality while reducing submission processing bottlenecks, offering actionable guidelines for conference organizers structuring high-volume peer-review pipelines.]]></Data>
        </Cell>
        <Cell>
          <Data ss:Type="String"><![CDATA[Nirand Pisutha-Arnond]]></Data>
        </Cell>
